// 2011's world map: interface 755 (worldmap), cache 786.
//
// The globe beside the minimap (toplevel:worldmap_icon, 548:179, fixed;
// toplevel_fullscreen:worldmap_button, 746:179, resizable; op1 "World Map",
// clientscript 334) swaps the whole game screen for the map: no game view,
// tabs or chat, in either frame (worldmap.if3 755:0 sizes itself to the
// canvas; Jagex Knowledge Base, Controls - The Basics, Wayback snapshot
// 2011-01-19; 2011 wiki, World map, rev 2011-05-10; YouTube 0PgitXjVAiU, "How
// to Play Runescape: Part 3", uploaded 2011-02-09, 05:06). Zoom, panning, the
// key, search, the overview and the area list run in the client.
//
// The server says where the player is: the map opens on the player, "even if
// you are in a dungeon (with a small number of exceptions)" (Jagex Knowledge
// Base, Controls - World Map View, snapshot 2011-05-20). worldmap_init
// (clientscript 1369) picks the map holding varc 622 and centres on it as 755
// loads, falling back to the surface; worldmap_overlay (clientscript 2045)
// draws "You are here" at varc 674. No clientscript writes either, so both
// are set before the IF_OPENTOP that loads the map.

[if_button1,toplevel:worldmap_icon] @worldmap_open;
[if_button1,toplevel_fullscreen:worldmap_button] @worldmap_open;

[label,worldmap_open]
// Inside Daemonheim the globe reads "Daemonheim Map" (varbit 6090,
// clientscript 334) and opens Daemonheim's own map, which is not ported.
if (%varplayerbit_6090 = 1) {
    return;
}
// "For obvious reasons, you will not be able to load the world map while you
// are in combat." (Jagex Knowledge Base, Controls - World Map View, Wayback
// snapshot 2011-05-20; 2011 wiki, World map, rev 2011-05-10). No source gives
// the combat refusal's text: the busy refusal stands in for it, unverified.
// That one is sourced (2011 wiki, Magic carpet, rev 2011-04-17).
if (p_finduid(uid) = false) {
    mes("Please finish what you are doing before opening the world map.");
    return;
}
if (add(%lastcombat, 8) > map_clock) {
    mes("Please finish what you are doing before opening the world map.");
    return;
}
%worldmap_focus = coord;
%worldmap_player_coord = coord;
if_opentop(worldmap);
// The key's option lines (below) send their ops only from the slots enabled
// here. void-634 enables 0-182, every line of the key; which range 2011 used
// is not known, and only the option lines (0-19) mean anything to the server.
if_setevents(worldmap:com_49, 0, 19, ^if_op1, 0, 0, false, false);
// Others see the player reading a map while it is open: "An animation has
// been added for when players are viewing the world map" (Jagex news 2419,
// 9 November 2009), an open map held up in both hands (YouTube kabOLpe353E,
// "New World Map Stance", uploaded 2009-11-10, 00:03-00:10). Seq 5354 is
// void-634's open_map (unused there; void-634 only): cache 786's 5354 hides
// the left hand, puts obj 9973 (a tan and black two-panel dummy model) in the
// right and loops from frame 8. In client B the player holds it up in front
// in both hands at chest height, the 2009 pose (2026-11-08).
anim(qip_watchtower_read_scroll, 0);

// The X at the top right (755:44, op1 "Close"). The client keeps every
// sub-interface across IF_OPENTOP, so the frame comes back with its tabs,
// orbs and chat as they were.
//
// In 2011 the screen went black with "Loading - please wait." and then the
// game view came back (YouTube 0PgitXjVAiU, 2011-02-09, 09:24 and 09:26).
// That is IF_OPENTOP's mode 2: the client rebuilds the scene behind its
// loading screen (ImageSourceTextureOp.method3619, client state 11). With
// mode 0, client B came back to a black game view and minimap that stayed
// black until the next map load (2026-11-08). Which mode Jagex's server sent
// is not known; void-634 sends 2 on close too (WorldMap.kt).
[if_button1,worldmap:close]
anim(null, 0);
if (getwindowmode = 1) {
    if_opentop_mode(toplevel, 2);
} else {
    if_opentop_mode(toplevel_fullscreen, 2);
}

// The key (755:49, "Show key"). Its order and four options live in varp 463,
// which the client reads and redraws from (if_setonvartransmit in
// worldmap_init): varbit 5367 the order, 6174-6177 the options. The client
// only relabels a clicked line (clientscript 1404 worldmap_key_toggle) or
// rebuilds the key in the next order (clientscript 692
// worldmap_keysort_button); the server keeps the varp. Varp 463 is protect=no
// (the map's buttons run unprotected, as every button on 548, 746 and 755
// does) and keeps cache 786's scope: not saved. No dated recording or Jagex
// page shows the key's order or options outliving a logout; void-634 saves
// them (persist = true).

// "Re-sort key" (755:54): Traditional, Alphabetical, Categorised, in turn
// (clientscripts 692 and 1839; YouTube 0PgitXjVAiU, 2011-02-09, 07:45 "Key
// order: Traditional", 08:30-08:38).
[if_button1,worldmap:sort_button]
%worldmap_keysort = ~worldmap_keysort_next(%worldmap_keysort);

// The option lines. script285 builds each as two clickable children of
// 755:49 and the client sends the clicked one's slot: "You are here" 0 and 1
// (varbit 6174), intra-map links 3 and 4 (6175), map labels 11 and 12 (6176),
// icon tooltips 14 and 16 (6177). Each is Hide/Show (Jagex Knowledge Base,
// Controls - World Map View, snapshot 2011-05-20: "options to turn some map
// features on or off"; YouTube 0PgitXjVAiU 08:11-08:22). void-634 answers
// only one child of each line (1, 4, 12, 16).
[if_button1,worldmap:com_49]
switch_int (last_slot) {
    case 0, 1 : %worldmap_yah_hidden = calc(1 - %worldmap_yah_hidden);
    case 3, 4 : %worldmap_samemaplinks_hidden = calc(1 - %worldmap_samemaplinks_hidden);
    case 11, 12 : %worldmap_textlabels_hidden = calc(1 - %worldmap_textlabels_hidden);
    case 14, 16 : %worldmap_tooltips_hidden = calc(1 - %worldmap_tooltips_hidden);
    case 18, 19 : ~worldmap_marker_clear; // "Clear your marker"
}

// The key's next order after $order, as clientscript 692
// (worldmap_keysort_button) rebuilds it on "Re-sort key": 0 Traditional, 1
// Alphabetical, 2 Categorised, then back to 0 (clientscript 1839). The
// server must pick the order the client has just drawn, or the varp's next
// update snaps the key back. This is RuneScript, not a shared .gs2 proc:
// every .gs2 proc is also compiled into the client's clientscripts, and a new
// one changes cache 786's archive 12 (crc_gate: 0xd4df1688, not 0xac5b8029),
// while 692 itself, which inlines the cycle, stays as the cache has it.
[proc,worldmap_keysort_next](int $order)(int)
switch_int ($order) {
    case 0 : return(1);
    case 1 : return(2);
    case default : return(0);
}

// "Your marker" (varp 1159, a coord, which cache 786 transmits). Double-
// clicking the map sets it, and a hint arrow points the way (Jagex news,
// Quiet Before the Swarm, 24 June 2010: a marker or waypoint, "a hint arrow
// to help you get there"; "Your Marker" since 19 July 2010, 2011 wiki,
// Update: Dungeoneering Update). The client draws it on the map from the
// varp (clientscript 2045, struct 972) and offers to clear it: "Clear marker"
// on it (755:42 op10, since 19 July 2010), "Clear your marker" in the key
// (script285 slots 18 and 19) and on the globe (op2, clientscript 334; since
// 25 August 2010, Jagex news, Conquest). Varp 1159 is protect=no (the map's
// buttons and CLICKWORLDMAP run unprotected) and keeps cache 786's scope: not
// saved. No dated recording or Jagex page shows a marker outliving a logout
// (void-634 saves it), so nothing re-sends its arrow at login.

// Entered from the engine, not from a trigger: CLICKWORLDMAP (rev-647 opcode
// 60), which the client sends on every press on the map, runs
// [proc,clickworldmap] by name with the pressed tile
// (rs-engine/src/handlers/client_status.rs), as WINDOW_STATUS runs
// [proc,switch_window_mode]. What makes a double-click is not known: no Jagex
// page, patch note or recording defines it. This is void-634's rule as the
// PRD reads it, void-634 only: the same tile again within 1 tick. (WorldMap.kt
// starts a 1-tick clock on each press, which taken strictly is the same tick;
// a quick double-click often straddles a tick, so the next one counts too.)
// The last press is kept in two of cache 786's server-only varps.
[proc,clickworldmap](coord $coord)
if ($coord = %worldmap_lastclick & sub(map_clock, %worldmap_lastclick_clock) <= 1) {
    ~worldmap_marker_set($coord);
}
%worldmap_lastclick = $coord;
%worldmap_lastclick_clock = map_clock;

[proc,worldmap_marker_set](coord $coord)
%playerdesign2_col = $coord;
~worldmap_marker_arrow;

// The marker's hint arrow, which blinks at the minimap's edge and over the
// tile, and stays when the player gets there (YouTube wI_EzW-balM,
// 2010-06-26, 00:08; bJKD9rL-rxc, 2010-07-10, 00:12-00:20; the client blinks
// every minimap hint arrow, Linkable_Sub13). Blue: icon 2 is the one index
// blue in all three hint sheets (hint_headicons, hint_mapmarkers,
// hint_mapedge). The client shows a tile arrow on the minimap only within
// range * 32 tiles, and 2010's marker showed from across the map, so the
// largest range. Its slot (7, clear of quests' 0) and height (0) are not
// known.
[proc,worldmap_marker_arrow]
hint_coord_slot(^worldmap_marker_slot, ^worldmap_marker_icon, ^worldmap_marker_offset, %playerdesign2_col, 0, ^worldmap_marker_range);

[proc,worldmap_marker_clear]
%playerdesign2_col = null;
hint_stop_slot(^worldmap_marker_slot);

[if_button2,toplevel:worldmap_icon] ~worldmap_marker_clear;
[if_button2,toplevel_fullscreen:worldmap_button] ~worldmap_marker_clear;
[if_button10,worldmap:arrow_waypoint] ~worldmap_marker_clear;
